---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const miles = assertion('seven-hundred-and-thirty-five-miles-of-channel')
const nameless = assertion('two-thirds-of-this-county-water-has-no-name')
const naming = assertion('a-name-is-what-runs-all-year')
const headwater = assertion('the-county-is-headwater-country')
const goes = assertion('where-all-of-it-goes')
const lima = assertion('lima-has-the-road-and-not-the-water')
const meet = assertion('the-river-is-where-they-meet')
const joins = assertion('the-auglaize-joins-the-maumee-and-it-is-no-longer-assumed')
const indiana = assertion('the-main-stem-is-named-for-a-river-in-indiana')
const three = assertion('three-questions-that-named-one-field')
const between = assertion('the-little-ottawa-joins-between-the-two-gauges')
const shawnee = assertion('fort-shawnee-has-more-water-than-lima')
const field = assertion('the-far-side-of-a-field')
const gulf = assertion('the-water-ends-in-the-gulf-of-saint-lawrence')
const ditches = assertion('the-ditches-on-the-other-side-have-no-name')
const sink = assertion('one-reach-reaches-nothing')
const swamp = assertion('forty-two-acres-of-the-great-black-swamp')
const ponds = assertion('most-of-the-standing-water-is-a-farm-pond')
const darke = assertion('a-creek-filed-in-a-county-it-never-touches')
const canal = assertion('the-canal-is-the-county-line')
const gauges = assertion('two-files-two-gauges-one-square-mile')

const spec = (a: typeof miles, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const classesSpec = spec(miles, 'Miles inside the county')
const orderSpec = spec(headwater, 'Miles inside the county')
const goesSpec = spec(goes, 'Miles inside the county')
const fieldSpec = spec(field, 'Miles of channel to the sea')
---

<Article slug="the-far-side-of-a-field">
  <p>
    The county's rivers have been in the corpus since the first day of it, and until now almost
    nothing about them had been read from a file that draws the water. Names came from a gazetteer,
    which gives a stream two coordinates and no course. Basins came from the watershed boundaries,
    which give a drainage area and never say which stream a stream joins first. Three separate
    entries said outright which field would settle them — the identifier of the reach immediately
    downstream — and named it years before there was any way to read it.
  </p>
  <p>
    There was a way all along. The federal flow network publishes that field, and every other
    attribute it computes, on a service with no key and no registration. Two requests return every
    channel in Allen County.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in the county</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={miles} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="classes"
    title="Allen County's channel, by what the file calls it"
    caption="Clipped to the county boundary. Artificial paths are lines drawn through standing water so the network joins up; the canal-or-ditch miles are one feature, and it is the Miami and Erie."
    columns={['Class', 'Miles']}
    rows={miles.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="classes" set:html={JSON.stringify(classesSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Seven hundred and thirty-five miles of watercourse and two thousand two hundred and twenty-six
    miles of road: three miles of road for every mile of water, in a county whose ground would not
    drain on its own. Two thirds of the water is dry for part of the year.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={nameless} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={naming} />
  <p>
    Those two figures are the same fact from either end. Somebody named the water that was there in
    August and left the rest alone, and the file that classes a channel as intermittent and the
    people who declined to name it were not consulting each other.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={headwater} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="order"
    title="Miles by Strahler stream order"
    caption="Order 1 is a channel with nothing flowing into it. Order 5 is the highest in the county, and it belongs to the Ottawa River."
    columns={['Order', 'Miles']}
    rows={headwater.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="order" set:html={JSON.stringify(orderSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Nothing large rises here because nothing large can. Four hundred square miles at the top of two
    basins produces first-order streams and very little else, and the largest river in the county
    is largest by a hundred miles of somebody else's drainage.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima} />

  <h2>Where it goes</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={goes} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="trunks"
    title="The first named river each mile of Allen County reaches"
    caption="Traced downstream from every reach to the first level path named for a trunk. A further 0.73 miles reaches nothing at all."
    columns={['River', 'Miles']}
    rows={goes.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="trunks" set:html={JSON.stringify(goesSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={meet} />
  <p>
    The gazetteer had put one coordinate at the end of two creeks and the head of a river, which is
    what a confluence looks like in a names file and is also what two creeks joining a few yards
    apart would look like. The network gives both creeks the same reach below them. They arrive
    together, and the reach they arrive at is the Ottawa River's first.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={three} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={joins} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={indiana} />
  <p>
    That last one is the trap in this source and it is worth stating plainly. The network offers a
    main stem as well as a reach, and the main stem is traced by drainage area rather than by name,
    so it walks up the largest tributary at every fork and keeps going under whatever the largest
    tributary is called. Rank the names on the path the Auglaize joins and the answer is a river in
    Indiana — a real river, in the right basin, that this water really does reach. The rule the
    corpus took from it is that an edge is read from the reach below and never from the path.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={between} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={shawnee} />

  <h2>The far side of a field</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={field} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="divide"
    title="Two headwaters, 858 metres apart"
    caption="Channel distance from each reach to the end of the network it belongs to. One ends in the Gulf of Saint Lawrence, the other in the Gulf of Mexico."
    columns={['Direction', 'Miles of channel']}
    rows={field.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="divide" set:html={JSON.stringify(fieldSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The continental divide crosses nine miles of one township in the south-east of this county and
    rises seventy-five feet across its whole length. Nothing on the ground marks it. What is on
    either side of it is a field, and the two furrows nearest the line are half a mile apart and
    seven hundred and fifty-two miles apart.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gulf} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ditches} />
  <p>
    The named thing on the Ohio River side is the watershed. The water in it is three ditches, all
    intermittent, all first order, all anonymous — and each of them begins inside Allen County and
    leaves it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={sink} />

  <h2>Standing water</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ponds} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={swamp} />
  <p>
    Four federal files describe wetness on this ground and they return 91,953 acres, 22,434, 704 and
    42. They are measuring four different things — what is wet, what was wet, what grew, and what a
    hydrographer chose to draw — and the smallest of them is a fair account of what a person walking
    the county would find standing in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Three things the files got wrong, or nearly</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={darke} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={canal} />
  <p>
    The canal is drawn twice by two arms of the same government, at 6.12 miles and 8.87, on lines
    that agree to about four metres. Most of the difference is a stretch north of Delphos where the
    canal and the county boundary are the same line to within a metre and a half — so the drawn
    canal crosses in and out of Allen County, and the answer to how much of it is here depends on
    which draughtsman you ask.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gauges} />
  <p>
    The last one is not a defect at all. Two files built for different purposes, decades apart, put
    the same two instruments on the same two drainages within a square mile — which is what it looks
    like when a source can be trusted, and worth recording as carefully as the failures.
  </p>
</Article>
